  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nRegional radar trends this afternoon displays a sizable area of\nprecipitation falling from Illinois, across the Ohio Valley, and\ninto England under the guise of a stout low pressure system.\nShowers rounding the western flank of this expansive disturbance\ncontinues to migrate into the Central Appalachians today into\ntonight, with low level temperatures allowing a rain and snow\nmixture to transpire at times across the forecast area. \n\nGround temperatures remain too warm for accumulations in the \nlower elevations, but will be more achievable along the higher\nterrain, which has already been noted by web camera time lapse\nimages in Pocahontas and Randolph Counties. Predominant snow\nshowers will prevail into the overnight hours along the\nmountains into Saturday morning, with the forecast remaining on\ntrack to see up to a few inches in accumulations along our\nwestern mountain slopes. The Winter Weather Advisory issued by\nthe previous shift remains unchanged in coverage and messaging.\nThis includes a brief period of freezing drizzle that could lead\nto a light glaze of ice come Saturday morning, in addition to\nlingering gradient winds on the upwards of 45 to 50 miles per\nhour, that could lead to blowing and drifting snow.\n\nThis impressive disturbance, which leaves behind a large clean\nup for the bulk of the northeast US, makes its mass exodus off\nthe New England coast overnight into Saturday. H85 streamlines\nshift out of the southwest throughout the day, cutting off any\nlingering lake enhanced moisture plumes drifting down into the\nmountains. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBrief mid-level ridging allots for a period of dry weather on\nSaturday into portions of Sunday. During which time, a surface\nwarm front lifts northward through the region, yielding slightly\nmoderating daytime temperatures closer to their climatological\nnorms. This will be short-lived, however, as the next low\npressure system takes aim for the Ohio River Valley for the end\nof the weekend. \n\nAn area of low pressure emerging off the lee of the Rockies will\nphase with southern stream moisture riding up from the Gulf,\nstirring up a strengthening system on Sunday. This disturbance\nwill encroach on the area beginning in the later half of Sunday,\nwith additional rain and mountain snow showers progged to round\nout the weekend and open up the start of the new work week.",
